<h1>Derrick Henry at 32: <em>Still Defying His Own Combine Profile</em></h1>
<div class="meta-row">
<span>📖 5 min read</span>
<span>🏈 Football</span>
<span>🔥 Ravens · Week 3</span>
</div>
<p class="dek">Derrick Henry has 214 rushing yards and four touchdowns through two games — and then got just five carries in the second half of Baltimore's 24-17 loss to the Saints, a game the Ravens led by 11 at halftime. The criticism was immediate. What's easy to lose in the frustration is just how unusual it is for a back built like Henry to still be doing this at 32.</p>
</div><div class="content">
<div class="toc">
<div class="toc-label">In This Article</div>
<a href="#usage">The Usage Controversy</a>
<a href="#combine">The 2016 Combine Profile</a>
<a href="#aging">Why He's Still Doing This at 32</a>
<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="usage">The Usage Controversy</h2>
<p>Henry opened the Saints game with 11 carries for 51 yards and a touchdown in the first half, helping Baltimore build a lead as wide as 14-3. In the second half, with the Ravens still in front, he got the ball just five more times for 17 yards as New Orleans erased the deficit and won 24-17. Hall of Fame receiver Terrell Owens called the decision "absurd" on his podcast, and former Ravens back Jamal Lewis was even more direct: "He's a workhorse. You're not going to have a workhorse like that for long, so you better use him."</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🏃</div>
<div class="stat-title">Derrick Henry — Through 2 Games, 2026</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Baltimore Ravens · Age 32</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">214</div><div class="label">Rushing Yards</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">4</div><div class="label">Total Touchdowns</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">5</div><div class="label">2nd-Half Carries, Wk 2</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Offensive coordinator Declan Doyle pointed to the Saints selling out to stop the run late, but the numbers tell an odd story either way: on a critical third-and-1 in the fourth quarter with the score tied, Baltimore threw deep instead of handing the ball to its 247-pound workhorse. It's not an isolated incident, either — Henry was given just five second-half carries in Baltimore's win-or-go-home Week 18 loss to Pittsburgh last season under similar circumstances.</p>
<h2 id="combine">The 2016 Combine Profile</h2>
<p>What makes Henry's continued production at 32 genuinely unusual is the specific type of athlete he tested as coming out of Alabama a decade ago. At nearly 6-foot-3 and 247 pounds, Henry ran a 4.54-second 40-yard dash at the 2016 combine — an outstanding time for a man his size — and posted a 37-inch vertical and a 10-foot-10 broad jump, both top-five marks among that year's running backs.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Measurable</th><th>Derrick Henry (2016)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Height / Weight</td><td>6'3", 247 lbs</td></tr>
<tr><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.54s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>37" (5th among RBs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broad Jump</td><td>10'10" (2nd among RBs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3-Cone Drill</td><td>7.20s (below average)</td></tr>
<tr><td>20-Yard Shuttle</td><td>4.38s (below average)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The profile was straight-line power and speed, not lateral agility — his 3-cone and shuttle times were both below average for the position. That combination described a bruising, downhill runner rather than a shifty change-of-direction back, and a decade of tape has borne that scouting report out almost exactly.</p>
