<h2 id="aging">Why He's Still Doing This at 32</h2>
<p>Running backs who rely on straight-line speed and pure size tend to age better than backs who depend on lateral quickness and short-area burst, because raw power and top-end speed decline more gradually than change-of-direction ability. Henry's game was never built on the kind of agility that erodes fastest with age — it was built on force production, which is exactly the quality he's still showing at a rate almost no other 32-year-old running back in NFL history has matched. He finished second in the league in rushing for the fourth straight year in 2025, and he's opened this season averaging 107 rushing yards per game.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Profile Still Holds</strong>
  A decade-old combine card correctly predicted the kind of back Henry would be: powerful, fast in a straight line, and not built on lateral agility. What it couldn't have predicted is how well that specific combination would hold up into his 30s — which is exactly why his own coaching staff sitting him for stretches continues to draw so much criticism.
</div>
